onfigfs group is missing — outcome 'skipped:configfs-group-missing'. Record data.serve_up true or false in EVERY receipt including skips. (e) Fixed schedule, no adaptivity, no retries: 50 iterations of 4 KiB ping-pong warmup (discarded); RTT at 64, 4096, 16384, 65536 bytes times 500 iterations with p50 and p99 per size; the allreduce-shaped simultaneous exchange at 8192, 16384, 65536 bytes times 500 (both sides write N bytes then read N bytes — ring 1024 times 4 KiB frames buffers this safely); throughput 256 MiB each direction; under 90 s wall total, whole run under a global timeout. (f) The receipt at results/receipts/usb4stream.json: one JSON object, step 'usb4stream', status ALWAYS 'pass' — this bench is evidence-gathering, not a campaign claim, so a mid-run abort is typed as data.outcome 'aborted:PHASE:ERRNO' rather than a fail status that would redden every later gate run; data carries outcome, per-size rtt and exchange p50/p99 fields, throughput both directions, ring_size and throttling as read from configfs, resolved device paths on both ends, open_count (assert exactly 1 per side), serve_up, and loop 'python' for honesty about syscall-loop overhead. (2) scripts/receipts-verify.py: add a 'usb4stream' entry to the BOUNDS table — outcome 'ok' requires open_count 1 and all schedule fields present; 'skipped:' and 'aborted:' outcomes require their reason text and pass. (3) docs/USB4STREAM-TRANSPORT.md — the decision memo: why the collective-library net-plugin route is REJECTED for the latency goal (it sits under the library's proxy and protocol stack, HopID scarcity shared with the network paths forces a multiplexer over one or two long-lived streams, and comm teardown on every serve restart is exactly the open/close pattern the wedge hazard forbids; 3-5 attended days to a fragile prototype, 2-4 attended weeks to overnight-trustworthy); why the real path is a 2-4 attended-day port of the reference doorbell-plus-progress-thread allreduce (the 105 microsecond reference bar) from verbs onto the stream device's read and write, with the receive path run by the progress thread; the morning trigger criteria (banked exchange p50 at 8-16 KiB at or under about 40 microseconds AND the bench matrix showing allreduce-dominated decode); the note that after a healthy night the first real run happens attended in the morning AFTER the pair is torn down (the serve-up skip is deliberate); and a drafted-not-filed issue body for the port. (4) tests/test_usb4stream_bench.py: py-compile both edited python files, assert the no-hardcoded-device-node invariant (no digit after tbstream in the bench source), assert the idempotence guard and the serve-up skip are present, and exercise the receipts checker's three outcome branches with synthetic receipt dicts. Boundaries: touch only bench/usb4stream-bench.py, docs/USB4STREAM-TRANSPORT.md, scripts/receipts-verify.py, and tests/.\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:588:      \"goal\": \"Measure whether the ROCm 10.0.0 substrate is viable for this fork on this hardware, without touching the 7.14 lane that served the night. Context: a complete aligned gfx1151/cp312 wheel set exists at https://stable.repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-next/ (verified 2026-08-30; wheels dated 2026-08-26; same upstream versions and the SAME triton git hash 4cff872c as the current pins, so the swap is a pure version-literal substitution) — but no upstream anywhere has run this engine on ROCm 10 on gfx1151, and two failure modes are unmeasured: whether ROCm 10's hipcc compiles the fork's HIP sources, and whether its HSA runtime binds to the in-tree KFD on this kernel. This lane answers both as DATA. Build a second image flashnext:rocm10 from the UNMODIFIED container/Containerfile using its existing build ARGs (ROCM_WHL, TORCH_PIN, TORCHVISION_PIN, TRITON_PIN, ROCM_DEVEL_PIN, DEVICE_PINS at container/Containerfile:48-65 — no recipe edit, so the banked container-recipe acceptance stays true): podman build --tag flashnext:rocm10 --file container/Containerfile --build-arg ROCM_WHL=https://stable.repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-next/ --build-arg 'TORCH_PIN=torch==2.13.0+rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'TORCHVISION_PIN=torchvision==0.28.0+rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'TRITON_PIN=triton==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'ROCM_DEVEL_PIN=rocm-sdk-devel==10.0.0' --build-arg 'DEVICE_PINS=amd-torch-device-gfx1151==2.13.0+rocm10.0.0 amd-torchvision-device-gfx1151==0.28.0+rocm10.0.0 rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151==10.0.0' . (a prefetched wheel set may exist under ~/.cache/flashnext-wheels/rocm10/). Then, on the coordinator only, run a minimal GPU binding check in that image (--device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host): torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).gcnArchName, a small fp16 and a small fp8-block matmul, and importing the engine package. Record the ROCm/HSA and KFD versions the runtime reports plus the running kernel release (a green probe on a 7.2.0 coordinator is strong but not conclusive for the pair; a red probe attributable to KFD must be re-checked on 7.2.2 before ROCm 10 is written off — say so in the notes field). Do not load the checkpoint, do not start a server, do not touch the worker. WRITE THE RECEIPT TO results/rocm10-probe.json — NOT under results/receipts/: scripts/receipts-verify.py fails the campaign gate on any fail-status receipt in that directory, and this lane exists to record an outcome, green or red, never to fail the campaign. The receipt carries {step:'rocm10-probe', status, ts, data:{build_ok, compile_seconds, torch, triton, rocm_sdk_core, gcn_arch, cuda_available, matmul_fp16_ok, matmul_fp8_block_ok, engine_import_ok, kernel_release, notes}} with status 'pass' whenever an outcome was recorded. Never remove or retag flashnext:dev; never edit container/, results/receipts/, or the worklist. If the night is running late this lane is the first thing the operator drops. Boundaries: touch only results/rocm10-probe.json.\",","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
